    <item>
      <title>Functionally divergent RHOA mutations drive peripheral T cell lymphoma development by engaging a common neomorphic pathway</title>
      <link>https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-026-79260-8</link>
      <content:encoded>
                &lt;p&gt;Nature Communications, Published online: 11 November 2026; &lt;a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-026-79260-8"&gt;doi:10.1038/s41467-026-79260-8&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;RHOA mutation variants, exerting opposite effects on canonical RHOA signaling, are associated with peripheral T-cell lymphomas (PTCL). Here, the authors show that different RHOA mutants share a neomorphic activity that activates the transcription factor NFAT to drive PTCL.</content:encoded>
